<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hunminjeongeum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1446">1446</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Correct Sounds for the Instruction of the People</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hunminjeongeum ('Correct Sounds for the Instruction of the People') is the document by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejong-the-great">King Sejong</a> of Joseon promulgated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangul">Korean alphabet</a>, now called Hangul, in 1446. The alphabet of 28 letters, created in 1443, was designed so that ordinary people could write their own language. The original explanatory edition, the Hunminjeongeum Haerye, rediscovered in 1940, is inscribed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s Memory of the World register.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hunminjeongeum.jpg" alt="Hunminjeongeum" width="320" height="363" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sejong the Great of Joseon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hunminjeongum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangul">Hangul</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> had used Hanja, Chinese characters, since antiquity. The difficulty of the script limited its use largely to the upper classes, and commoners were mostly illiterate. Hanja also suits Korean poorly, since the two languages belong to different families and differ in structure: Classical Chinese puts the verb before its object, Middle Korean after it.</p><p>Because of a lack of records it is unknown when work on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangul">Hangul</a> began or how it proceeded. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejong-the-great">King Sejong</a> the Great, who reigned from 1418 to 1450, was responsible for it and deeply involved. Hangul was introduced to his court, probably in a mostly complete form, in the twelfth month of 1443 by the Korean calendar, between about 30 December 1443 and 28 January 1444. Work then began on applying the script and preparing official documentation. The base text claims publication in the ninth month of 1446. The postface of the commentary is dated to the first ten days of that month, which on the tenth day is 9 October, the date observed as Hangul Day in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The base text, written by Sejong in Classical Chinese, has two parts: a short preface and a description of the alphabet, occupying four leaves in the Haerye edition. The preface says that the sounds of the country's language differ from those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Kingdom</a>, so that ignorant people with something to say were often unable to express it. Sejong writes that he has newly designed twenty-eight letters for everyone to practise at their ease and use daily.</p><p>It then introduces the 17 consonants and 11 vowels, each with its shape and a Hanja character showing its sound, and sets out orthographic rules. Initial consonants can serve as final ones, consonants and vowels combine into syllables, and tone is marked by dots at the left of a character: none for the level tone, one for the departing tone and two for the rising tone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The Hunminjeongeum Haerye, a commentary written separately by a group of scholars led by Chŏng Inji, is believed to be closest to the text as published in 1446. It was lost and forgotten, possibly by the early 16th century, and was rediscovered in 1940 in what is called the Kansong copy. That copy became a National Treasure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> on 20 December 1962 and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s Memory of the World Register in 1997. A second, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangju">Sangju</a> copy was discovered in 2008.</p><p>Other versions include the text in the Veritable Records of Sejong, and Eonhae editions translated into Korean mixed script, the oldest known variant of which appears in the Wŏrin sŏkpo of 1459.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hunminjeongeum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1443 King Sejong created a script of 28 letters, promulgated in 1446 as the <b>Hunminjeongeum</b> ('Correct Sounds for the Instruction of the People'), so that ordinary people could learn to write their own language.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Sejong the Great of Joseon published the <b>Hunminjeongeum</b>, introducing a new phonetic script now called Hangul.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-9">9 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
